module extdeps.posix.shell_command_language


data posix_authority_duplication_note: String = "THE LOCATOR IS DECLARED IN FIVE MODULES AND THE CONSOLIDATION WAS ATTEMPTED, EXECUTED, AND REVERTED — a measured incompatibility, not an unexamined duplication (cursor review 48163 and the CI receipt that refuted its fix, 2026-08-04). The review is CORRECT that this locator appears verbatim in extdeps.shell, extdeps.shell.exec, extdeps.transports.shell, extdeps.process.posix_exit and here, and correct that consume-never-fork is the governing rule. The consolidation was built the right way round — this subject module as the authority, the four consumers aliasing it, so a transport module never owns the specification citation its own subject module consumes — and it reduced the corpus from five copies to one. IT ALSO TURNED v2.lens.mandatory_tag's corpus_live_clean_tree_wall_holds RED, measured by execution rather than predicted: the wall projects each extdeps module's extdeps_external_authority_anchor and reads a scheme identity off it, and that projection resolves a LITERAL construction, not an alias to one. So a module whose anchor is an alias projects no readable scheme and counts as a violation. THIS IS THE THIRD INDEPENDENT REDISCOVERY OF A MEASURED CONSTRAINT THE CORPUS ALREADY RECORDS, and citing it is the point of this sentence (extdeps.pin pin_citation_note; fierce-heron-301 supplied the pointer, 2026-08-04). That note says the same thing from reviews 44329 and 44866: a review proposed referencing a sibling module's anchor instead of re-spelling it, the remedy is NOT AVAILABLE because src/v2/lens/mandatory_tag reads the anchor structurally from the Node tree, and an aliased declaration reds corpus_live_clean_tree_wall_holds. It even says it is carried forward because the constraint is measured and would otherwise be rediscovered the hard way. It was, here, by turning CI red. A constraint that is documented in a carrier note but enforced nowhere gets rediscovered once per lane that trips it, and three rediscoveries is the argument for resolving it rather than documenting it a fourth time. THE FINDING, which is worth more than the cleanup: the mandatory-tag corpus scan STRUCTURALLY REQUIRES the duplication section 3 forbids. As mechanized today, 'every extdeps module declares its own anchor' is satisfiable only by re-declaring the literal per module, so any two modules governed by one upstream must copy its URI. That is a real conflict between two live rules and it is NOT mine to resolve inside a review round: it turns on whether the anchor is a CITATION VALUE (one authority, shared) or a PER-MODULE DECLARATION of which upstream governs this module (inherently repeated, and then five copies are five different facts that share a string). Escalated; the wall stays green meanwhile. THE CORPUS ALREADY LEANS ONE WAY, and it would be dishonest to escalate as though the question were open at both ends: pin_citation_note resolves its own instance toward PER-MODULE CITATION — its two anchors are 'two distinct CITATIONS, not one fact spelled twice' — A SECOND CORROBORATING INSTANCE WAS CLAIMED HERE AND IS WITHDRAWN (2026-08-05, retracted by its own author). This row previously cited fierce-heron-301's SPICE and ngspice carriers as reaching the same reading independently, on the grounds that two modules spelling one locator represented a dialect citation and a simulator's own subject anchor — genuinely different facts about different subjects. That is FALSE, and the reason is this file's own rule violated one lane over: the SPICE carrier was listing the ngspice manual as a further_citation while that same locator is the ngspice subject module's authority anchor, which is exactly the independently-governed-subject-as-citation error bash_implementation_versus_specification_note forbids. Review 48361 showed that the SPICE occurrence was itself invalid; gunbc#7803 removes that occurrence rather than defending it, so the pair cannot corroborate per-module duplication. THE TENSE HERE IS DELIBERATE AND WAS CORRECTED (operator review, 2026-08-05): an earlier revision of this sentence read 'the citation was deleted rather than defended, and the locator now appears once', which is a PRESENT-TENSE claim about a state that exists only on an OPEN BRANCH — not in this tree and not on main. That is the same stale-authority class this whole note is about, committed by the retraction correcting it: a carrier that asserts an unlanded branch's state as current is false the moment that branch is abandoned or rewritten, and nothing in this file would ever detect it. What is durable is the EVIDENTIARY fact — the review found the occurrence invalid and it was not defended — so that is what the sentence now carries, with the removal attributed to the pull request that performs it rather than narrated as an accomplished fact of the corpus. So the pair never spelled one locator for a good reason; it spelled it for a bad one. The corroboration is retracted and the per-module-citation reading rests on pin_citation_note alone. THE POSIX CASE IS THE WEAKEST ONE FOR THAT READING and that is exactly why it is the one worth deciding: all five modules cite this document for the SAME reason — shell command semantics — so unlike pin's two anchors, there is no second fact to point at. The SPICE/ngspice pair is deliberately NOT named in that contrast, because the retraction above moved it to the other side of the argument: it was withdrawn precisely for failing to be a second fact, so citing it here as a case where a second fact existed would re-assert on one line what the same row retracts on another. If per-module citation is the rule, then cursor review 48163's finding was a false positive on a text coincidence and the five copies are correct. If it is not, the projection has to resolve aliases before any of them can be consolidated. DISSOLVE-ON: the corpus-scan projection resolves an aliased anchor to its literal, or the anchor's role is settled as per-module declaration and this note records that instead. Reverting was the only landing state that did not either ship a red wall or leave the consolidation half-applied — the four modules keep their literals and so does this one."

data extdeps_external_authority_anchor: extdeps.external_authority.ExternalAuthority = extdeps.external_authority.ExternalAuthority {
  uri: extdeps.uri.Uri {
    scheme: extdeps.uri.Https
    locator: "pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799/utilities/V3_chap02.html"
  }
}

data posix_shell_subject_note: String = "THE POSIX SHELL COMMAND LANGUAGE, as its own subject rather than as a citation of GNU Bash (review relayed through the operator, 2026-08-04). It was previously stored as a further_citation on the GNU Bash ExternalModelScope, while the Bash carrier's own prose said the two were independently governed entities — so the file asserted the decomposition and then violated it one declaration later. DESIGN section 3's external-upstream decomposition rules on exactly this: multiple citations may ground ONE subject, but a second INDEPENDENTLY GOVERNED subject is not a citation of the first. The Open Group governs this specification; the GNU project governs Bash; neither versions with the other, and a change to one must not require editing the other's module — the Chromium/Safari test from that section. WHAT THIS SUBJECT IS: the Shell Command Language chapter of IEEE Std 1003.1-2017 (POSIX.1-2017), the shell syntax and semantics an implementation may conform to. It is NOT registered as a language target today and this module makes no such claim; it exists so that Bash's conformance can be stated as a relation between two named subjects instead of being smuggled into one scope. If a POSIX-shell target is ever claimed in its own right — a real possibility, since extdeps.shell.exec already routes shell.Exec.Check through 'sh -c' rather than through bash — it registers against THIS declaration and needs no new grounding."

type PosixShellCommandLanguage
  = | PosixShellCommandLanguageSpecification

data posix_shell_command_language: PosixShellCommandLanguage = PosixShellCommandLanguageSpecification

data posix_shell_model_scope: extdeps.external_authority.ExternalModelScope = extdeps.external_authority.ExternalModelScope {
  subject: extdeps.external_authority.ExternalSubjectRef {
    declaration: std.decl_ref.DeclarationRef {
      module_path: "extdeps.posix.shell_command_language",
      decl_name: "posix_shell_command_language",
      field: std.decl_ref.WholeDeclaration
    }
  },
  first_citation: extdeps_external_authority_anchor,
  further_citations: []
}

data extdeps_model_scope: extdeps.external_authority.ExternalModelScope = posix_shell_model_scope

data posix_single_quote_citation_note: String = "IEEE 1003.1-2017 section 2.2.2 (Single-Quotes): 'A single-quote shall preserve the literal value of each character within the single-quotes. A single-quote cannot occur within single-quotes.' That sentence is a complete correctness proof for posix_single_quote's encoding -- wrap the word in single quotes, and represent each embedded single quote as the four characters quote-backslash-quote-quote (close the quoting, emit an escaped literal quote via backslash, reopen the quoting) -- for every byte sequence, with no metacharacter table and no word-expansion model. This function moved here from v2.extdeps.languages.bash bash_single_quote (operator finding, 2026-08-19): the encoding is a POSIX shell command language fact, not a bash-specific one -- GNU Bash and gunbc.remote_shell_command both cite it for the same reason, so the rule has one home matching its one cited authority, and a bash-specific change to Bash's own emitter can no longer silently invalidate a POSIX claim made outside that module."

fn posix_single_quote(spelling: String) -> String {
  concat("'", concat(replace(spelling, "'", "'\\''"), "'"))
}
